        "id": 1087703
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-25T23:54:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do you want an @IntDef for these?\n\nAlso, what\u0027s the rationale for naming these scores vague values like \"high\", \"medium\", etc. when the comments say that they each have a more precise meaning? Are you expecting to assign SCORE_MEDIUM to something that\u0027s not necessarily same-offset-but-maybe-not-the-same-zone in the future?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 112,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 112,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "35131d76a39ab4fb36d22a22a4b590d2ac33d704",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "984c9437_992409dc",
        "filename": "src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/nitz/service/TimeZoneDetectionService.java",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 112,
      "author": {
        "id": 1038039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-11-05T14:04:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "RE IntDef: IntDef seems good for enums (and bit masks) where you want distinct values and type safety. It usually makes no sense with an enum to say x \u003e y (e.g. RED \u003e BLUE). This case is also a  score / ranking: higher values are worth more than lower ones, and there\u0027s the threshold constant too.\n\nRE rationale:\nShort answer, yes....?\n\nBy keeping the score abstract it could be extended in future. It seemed slightly nicer to have constants for the scores rather than just hardcoding 0, 1, 2, 3; then I could document their meaning with examples. I agree the names chosen are poor, but the only alternative I could think of was SCORE_0, SCORE_1, etc. which seemed worse at the time. Happy to change it if like.\n\nThe scoring is already slightly abstract, as you can see in scoreSuggestion(). It looks at the \"match type\" and \"quality\" properties to arrive at a score. The quality comparisons are already conflating different match types (e.g. \"country\" and \"country + NITZ\" matches). i.e. we treat a \"single zone\" answer the same regardless of whether it was derived using \"only country\" or \"country + NITZ\".",
      "parentUuid": "b848dd2e_9994c3d1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 112,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 112,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "35131d76a39ab4fb36d22a22a4b590d2ac33d704",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
